{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>And it shall growl over it.</b> Over Israel." "<b>Like the roaring of the sea.</b> For then the light of the world will grow dark, and it is written: חשכת מים (Psalm 18:12)." "And the sense of <b>and one looks to the land</b> is that they will be on land and not on sea, and darkness comes by way of metaphor for the distresses of the heart. Some say that ונבט is from the nifal binyan, but what is closer to me is that it is from the heavy [piel] binyan, with dagesh, and it refers back to Israel as I said regarding the word עליו." "<b>Distress and light.</b> Some say the sun and the moon, and others said the moon and the sun, for the moon is narrow/opposite the sun. And Rabbi Adoniah says that the letter heh is missing, as if he said צהר, for the letters yod heh vav alef are sometimes superfluous and are also found deficient. But he erred, for heh is not one of the letters of prolongation, nor is it elided except in the final position, where it is interchangeable with alef in pronunciation. And the correct view in my eyes is to take it according to its plain sense, that צר is like צר ומצוק מצאוני (Psalm 119:143), or it may be an adjective modifying the noun, and the sense is that the land of Israel will grow dark because of distress, and also because of the light that will grow dark at the time of its destruction, for it will ascend [?] — how the light of the sun is covered." "<b>In its clouds.</b> Like יערף מזבחתם (Hosea 10:2), and close to the derivation of וערפתו (Exodus 13:13)."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {3 "The phrase 'כי יעלה איך' is textually difficult; translated hyper-literally. The word איך may be a corruption."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:52:21.965579768Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}